    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Who Will Bring the Cleaning Supplies?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Interpret simple charts and graphs related to texts.
reading_2: Analyze characters' actions and motives in stories.
phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret data in charts about community activities (e.g., 'Most students like to clean the garden.').
Can analyze actions and motives of characters in short stories about supporting the community (e.g., 'She is happy because she will participate in the clean-up campaign.').
Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ɪ/ in 'bring' and /iː/ in 'clean').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different community cleanup activities (e.g., students picking up trash in a park, cleaning streets, recycling). Students quickly identify activities using vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., "clean," "park," "trash," "recycling," "bags"). Teacher asks, "What do we need for these cleanups?" to elicit "supplies."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a short text for specific information by demonstrating with a simple paragraph about a cleanup event. For example, "I want to find out 'who needs gloves.' I will look for the word 'gloves' or 'wear'." The teacher also models the pronunciation of target words with short and long vowel sounds like /ɪ/ in 'bring' and /iː/ in 'clean', /æ/ in 'bag' and /eɪ/ in 'safe', highlighting the difference and word stress on imperative commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'pick up the trash,' what should you do?" (Expected: show picking up motion).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'bring' a short /ɪ/ or long /iː/ sound?" (Expected: short /ɪ/).</li></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher distributes a short story titled "The Community Cleanup Team" (approximately 100-120 words), which includes a simple chart showing tasks and volunteers for a community project. The text describes a group of friends (characters) organizing a cleanup, mentioning who will bring specific supplies and what tasks they will do on Saturday or next week. It includes modal verbs (should, can, must) and future tense ('We will clean...').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short narrative excerpt with an embedded simple bar chart, printed on a worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets written information on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the story about?" "When will the cleanup happen?" (Students scan for general understanding).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements and mark them True or False based on the text (e.g., "The park is very clean. (False)").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match volunteers to the supplies they will bring (e.g., "Maria - trash bags," "Pedro - gloves").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension; identifying characters' actions and motives; interpreting charts; using modal verbs (can, should, must), future tense (will), and imperatives (Bring, Collect) within context. Focus on vocabulary: community, supplies, trash, tasks, group, volunteers, clean, bring, pick up, collect, help, organize, bags, recycling, responsibility.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> The teacher provides a paragraph from the story with key vocabulary words (e.g., 'supplies,' 'volunteers,' 'clean,' 'responsibility') or verbs (e.g., 'bring,' 'collect') removed. Students read the full story again and fill in the blanks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled (Chart Interpretation Questions):</b> Students answer specific questions based on the chart in the story. For example, "According to the chart, which task has the most volunteers?" "Who will collect recycling?" "What supplies must the 'garden team' bring?" They identify short and long vowel sounds from specific words found in the chart (e.g., `team` /iː/, `bags` /æ/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Character Analysis Discussion):</b> In pairs, students discuss a character's actions and motives from the story. "Why is Ana happy to help clean the park?" "What does Carlos think they should do first?" (Encourage answers like, "Ana is happy because she will participate. Carlos thinks they should collect the big trash first."). Teacher circulates, monitoring correct usage of modal verbs and future tense.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Report-back.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a slightly different simple chart or graph showing volunteer preferences for cleanup tasks (e.g., "Most students like to clean the garden," "Few students want to collect trash"). They must collectively read and interpret the chart, discussing the data using target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "Our chart shows that many people want to clean the garden. We should ask them to bring garden tools."). They must also answer the question, "Who will bring the cleaning supplies for the most popular task?" based on their chart's data.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After interpreting, each group briefly reports their findings and suggestions to the class (e.g., "In our group, we will clean the park because it has the most volunteers. We should bring bags and gloves."). This activity promotes participation in group discussions on community actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing interpretation of data, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion (reporting findings from the chart and making suggestions for supplies).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group report-backs and a short individual quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short worksheet with two sections:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. A simple chart interpretation task (e.g., "Based on this chart, which area needs the most helpers?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. A question about character motives from the original story (e.g., "Why did the main character decide to organize the cleanup project?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Identify short /ɪ/ and long /iː/ sounds in a list of words from the text (e.g., "Is 'clean' /ɪ/ or /iː/?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in interpreting data from the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in analyzing characters' actions and motives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of short and long vowel sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'volunteers,' 'tasks,' 'responsibility,' 'supplies').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned about interpreting charts today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you understand why characters act in a story about helping the community?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the story or chart was easy for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was challenging, and how can you improve your reading skills?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading activities effectively engaged students in interpreting data and analyzing characters' actions. The phonemic awareness practice was integrated well. Some students needed more scaffolding to connect motives directly to actions. Next time, I will provide more sentence starters for character analysis discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This reading lesson provided the content and context for describing community actions and plans. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will actively use the vocabulary and grammatical structures from the story and charts to discuss and plan their own hypothetical community cleanup, building on their comprehension of the text and data.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>